            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Aspasia of Miletus</strong><br>\nAspasia was born around 470 BCE in the ancient Greek city of Miletus. She was known for her intelligence and wit and moved to Athens as an adult. In Athens, she became a companion to Pericles, one of the most influential statesmen of the time. Although she was not an Athenian citizen, Aspasia played a significant role in the city's political and cultural life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Life in Athens</strong><br>\nUpon her arrival in Athens, Aspasia quickly became part of the city's intellectual circles. She was noted for hosting gatherings where leading philosophers, politicians, and writers would convene. Her home was a center of Athenian intellectual activity, and she influenced many prominent thinkers, including Socrates.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Pericles</strong><br>\nAspasia's relationship with Pericles was both personal and profound. Pericles was a leading figure in Athenian politics, and their partnership was unique for the era because Aspasia was treated as a social equal, despite women generally having lower status. Their union was significant enough to draw criticism and satire from contemporary playwrights and poets.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
