      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Athenian Society</strong><br>\nAspasia's impact on Athenian society was noteworthy. She was known for her involvement in shaping cultural and political discourse, a rare position for a woman of her time. Her influence extended to the public decisions of Pericles, although the exact nature of her contributions is subject to historical debate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Peloponnesian War</strong><br>\nDuring the Peloponnesian War, Aspasia faced personal challenges due to her close association with Pericles. As Athens suffered from the Spartan threat and internal strife, Aspasia was criticized by Pericles' political opponents, who targeted her in an attempt to undermine his authority.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legal Challenges</strong><br>\nAspasia was once charged with impiety, a serious accusation in Athens that reflected the city's intense political rivalries. Pericles defended her, and she was acquitted. This incident demonstrated the precarious position of influential women in Athenian politics and how personal relationships could become entangled with public matters.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
