          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Aspasia and Socrates</strong><br>\nThe philosopher Socrates is said to have admired Aspasia for her knowledge and intellectual capacity. She is attributed by some ancient sources to have taught him rhetoric. This association with Socrates further emphasizes her remarkable role in a society where women were rarely educated or given a voice in the public sphere.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aspasia's Later Years</strong><br>\nAfter Pericles died in 429 BCE, little is known about Aspasia's later life. It is speculated that she continued to be active in Athenian intellectual circles and may have married another prominent Athenian after Pericles's death. Her later years, however, remain mostly a mystery.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Aspasia's Legacy</strong><br>\nAspasia's legacy survives through the works of ancient historians and playwrights, who documented her life and times, albeit often with a mix of respect and scandal. She remains a figure of fascination for historians as a woman who defied the conventions of her time to leave a mark on classical Greek history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
